1. Basic Nmap commands
Command	Use
nmap --version	Nmap version check
nmap -h	Help options
nmap 127.0.0.1	Basic scan
nmap -v 127.0.0.1	Detailed scan progress
nmap -vv 127.0.0.1	More detailed progress
nmap -F 127.0.0.1	Fast scan
nmap -n 127.0.0.1	DNS lookup skip
nmap -Pn 127.0.0.1	Host discovery skip; assume target is up

2. Port scanning commands
Command	Use
nmap -p 22 127.0.0.1	Port 22 scan
nmap -p 80 127.0.0.1	Port 80 scan
nmap -p 443 127.0.0.1	Port 443 scan
nmap -p 22,80,443 127.0.0.1	Multiple ports scan
nmap -p 1-1000 127.0.0.1	Ports 1 to 1000
nmap -p- 127.0.0.1	All TCP ports
nmap --top-ports 20 127.0.0.1	Top 20 common ports
nmap -p 80-100 127.0.0.1	Ports 80 to 100

3. Service and OS detection
Command	Use
nmap -sV 127.0.0.1	Service version detection
nmap -sV --version-light 127.0.0.1	Lighter version detection
nmap -O 127.0.0.1	OS detection
sudo nmap -O 127.0.0.1	OS detection with privileges
sudo nmap -A 127.0.0.1	OS, version, scripts and traceroute
nmap -sC 127.0.0.1	Default NSE scripts
nmap -sT 127.0.0.1	TCP connect scan
sudo nmap -sS 127.0.0.1	TCP SYN scan

-A and -sC can run additional probes. Use them only on your own system or authorized lab.8

4. Network discovery commands
Command	Use
ip a	Own IP address find
ip route	Network route check
nmap -sn 127.0.0.1	Host discovery
nmap -sn 192.168.1.0/24	Local network devices discover
nmap -sL 192.168.1.0/24	List target IP addresses
nmap -R 127.0.0.1	Reverse DNS lookup
nmap --traceroute 127.0.0.1	Network route trace

Note: 192.168.1.0/24 is an example network. Ungaloda actual network range use pannunga.

5. UDP scanning commands
UDP scan-ku sudo use pannunga.
Command	Use
sudo nmap -sU 127.0.0.1	UDP scan
sudo nmap -sU -p 53 127.0.0.1	UDP port 53 scan
sudo nmap -sU -p 161 127.0.0.1	UDP port 161 scan
sudo nmap -sU --top-ports 20 127.0.0.1	Top 20 UDP ports

- UDP 53 → DNS
- UDP 161 → SNMP
